Reclassify from 'bug/low' to 'enhancement' (defensive hardening). There is no defect today: at mount state.sfx is {cue:null,n:0}, the effect's n!==lastSfx guard is false, and the cue is additionally null-guarded; the intro dialog is already gated on the current section. Drop the title's claim that these effects 'fire based on currentSection not isfocus' — it does not match the code.

If you still want the cheap insurance against a future engine change that emits an sfx during createRun/generateDay, do NOT use the proposed one-liner verbatim (it would replay an offscreen cue on first focus). Instead update the marker unconditionally and only PLAY when focused:

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!state.sfx || state.sfx.n === lastSfx.current) return;
    lastSfx.current = state.sfx.n;            // advance regardless so we never replay
    if (isfocus && state.sfx.cue) playSFX(state.sfx.cue);
  }, [state.sfx, isfocus, playSFX]);

Note isfocus is now a dependency. This guarantees an offscreen cue is swallowed (not queued) and the first on-focus cue isn't a stale replay. A cleaner long-term fix is to not run a live run for offscreen sections at all (lazy-init the reducer on first focus), which also removes the Date.now() seed being burned on a never-played run — but that is a larger refactor than this S-effort guard.

Keep the proposed post-generation guarantee, but tighten it. In generateDay, after building `shelf`, test feasibility against the day's actual budget and a real profit margin, then inject if it fails:

  const wantCats2 = wants.map(w => w.category);
  const bonusByCat = Object.fromEntries(wants.map(w => [w.category, w.bonus]));
  const hasFlip = shelf.some(s => {
    if (!wantCats2.includes(s.category)) return false;
    if (s.floor > state.gold) return false;
    return round(s.value * (1 + bonusByCat[s.category])) - s.floor > 0;
  });
  if (!hasFlip) {
    // pick the cheapest catalog item whose category is wanted; instance+price it; replace the priciest shelf slot
    const cheapWanted = ITEMS.filter(i => wantCats2.includes(i.category)).sort((a,b)=>a.base-b.base)[0]
                     || ITEMS.filter(i => ["Food","Potion","Tool"].includes(i.category)).sort((a,b)=>a.base-b.base)[0];
    // if the cheapest wanted base still won't clear budget, ALSO force its category into wants
    const inst = priceShelfItem(makeInstance(state, cheapWanted, rng), state, event);
    if (!wantCats2.includes(inst.category)) {
      wants.push({ category: inst.category, bonus: round((rng.range(0.55,0.9)+wantBonus)*100)/100 });
    }
    shelf[shelf.indexOf(shelf.reduce((m,s)=>s.floor>m.floor?s:m))] = inst;
  }

Notes / corrections to the original rec: (1) Use the DAY'S gold (`state.gold`) and a >0 margin exactly as written — correct. (2) Don't hard-code the magic 0.7*startGold (~98) threshold; that's day-1-specific and breaks for later days — test against actual `state.gold` instead. (3) The original's "force-swap a cheap WANTED instance" can fail if all wanted categories are expensive event-locked sets (e.g. collector event = Curio/Relic only, where the cheapest is ancient-coin base 90 — fine — but gemRush = Gem/Trinket where the cheapest, silver-ring base 60, IS a Trinket so it works; verify per-event). Adding the "also push its category into wants" fallback above makes the guarantee robust regardless of event. (4) Severity adjusted from high to MEDIUM: it's a genuine bug affecting only ~2.7% of day-1 cold opens and is not a softlock (the player can still pay day-1 rent and continue), so player impact is real but bounded; the fix is genuinely S-effort.

Defer run creation AND re-seed at first entry; the existing RESTART action makes this clean.

1) Keep the lazy initializer cheap so nothing heavy runs at mount. Either (a) gate generateDay behind a started flag — give createRun a phase like 'idle' that skips generateDay until a START dispatch, or (b) keep the current createRun but treat the at-mount state as throwaway and overwrite it on first focus.

2) Re-seed on first focus so the run feels fresh and is NOT frozen at page-load time:
   const everFocused = useRef(false);
   useEffect(() => {
     if (isfocus && !everFocused.current) {
       everFocused.current = true;
       dispatch({ type: 'RESTART', seed: (Date.now() >>> 0) || 1 });
     }
   }, [isfocus]);
   (Move the Date.now() capture out of seedRef-at-mount and into this first-focus moment.)

3) Stop the off-screen render/animation cost regardless: gate the heavy subtree with `{isfocus || everFocused.current ? <ShopBody/> : <ShopPlaceholder/>}` so the portrait `bob`/`flameFlicker` infinite animations and the staggered shelf cards don't mount/animate until the player has reached the section. This also guarantees the restock `wareIn` stagger (Shop.jsx:370) plays on entry, not before.

4) Cheap complementary win at the Home level: add `content-visibility: auto` (or toggle `display:none` on non-current sections) in Home.module.css for off-screen sections so the browser skips rendering/animation work for ALL off-screen sections, not just the Shop — this addresses the root architectural cause (every section painted at once) rather than only the symptom.
